     approval of two firms as "co-lead counsel."  When it joined with a second

     "group" to compete with an institution to be lead plaintiff, the group

     increased, without explanation, the number of proposed co-lead counsel to

     three firms, adding the firm representing the second group.  Ultimately,

     the institution negotiated with three members of the large group a joint

     lead plaintiff proposal, providing for two firms as lead counsel, which the

     court accepted.  This suggests that unless there has been active, effective

     client participation in the process, it is possible that the counsel

     arrangement may simply reflect bargaining among lawyers for their own stake

     in the case, and not serve the best interests of the class. <(14)>
                              

